macOS 27+ includes an undocumented feature flag that lets iPhone and iPad mirroring run in its own window. It is a small but welcome change: the mirrored device can now sit alongside your apps instead of taking over the usual AirPlay experience.

Because this is an undocumented system setting, it may change or disappear in a future macOS update. Use it only if you are comfortable enabling experimental features.

<PostImage
	src={airplayWindow}
	alt="An iPhone mirrored in its own window on a macOS desktop"
	width={3440}
	height={1440}
	caption="An iPhone mirrored in a standalone macOS window."
/>

## Enable windowed mirroring

Create the feature-flags directory, then enable the AirPlay receiver window setting:

<Command delay={24} command="sudo mkdir -p /Library/Preferences/FeatureFlags/Domain" />

<Command
	delay={24}
	command="sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/FeatureFlags/Domain/ControlCenter APReceiverInWindow -dict Enabled -bool true"
/>

Restart your Mac for the change to take effect. After it starts again, mirror an iPhone or iPad as usual and macOS will open the receiver in a separate window.

## Restore the previous behavior

To remove the feature flag and return to the previous AirPlay behavior, run:

<Command
	delay={24}
	command="sudo defaults delete /Library/Preferences/FeatureFlags/Domain/ControlCenter APReceiverInWindow"
/>

Restart your Mac once more to apply the rollback.
